## Performing Binomial Thinning

In our case we want to thin say the largest peak, so the SNP will affect one peak each
over say 'eff_width' =10 with an effect_size of 1.5 see David Gerard BMC paper for detail


```{r}
  Y= count.data
  eff_width=10
  L=2
  effect_size=1.5
  P       <- ncol(Y)
  magnitude <- colMeans(Y)
  ord       <- order(magnitude, decreasing = TRUE)
  starts    <- integer(0)
  for (p in ord) {
    s <- min(p, P - eff_width + 1L)
    if (length(starts) == 0 || all(abs(s - starts) >= eff_width))
      starts <- c(starts, s)
    if (length(starts) == L) break
  }
  if (length(starts) < L) return(NULL)

  lf <- lapply(starts, function(s) {
    v <- numeric(P)
    v[(s- eff_width/2):(s + eff_width/2 - 1L)] <- effect_size
    v
  })
  B <- do.call(cbind, lf)          
  
  plot(lf[[1]], type="l")
  lines(lf[[2]], col="red")
```
Here you see where causal SNP 1 and 2 will affect the counts


Now getting some SNP (for the sake of ease I will use the data from susie package, for good simulation use MAF>5% ) and make it "SNP like"

Here we have L=2 causal SNP
```{r}
L=2
genotype= abs(N3finemapping$X[1:N,])
genotype=ifelse(genotype<.5, 0,genotype)
genotype=ifelse(genotype>=.5 & genotype< 1.5 , 1,genotype) 

genotype=ifelse(genotype>=1.5  , 2,genotype) 
true_pos <- sample(seq_len(ncol(genotype)), size = L)
```

Finally thinning the data

```{r}
  Ynew <- t(thin_base(mat       =  t(Y),
                      designmat = genotype[, true_pos, drop = FALSE],
                      coefmat   = B))
```


